<p>The last passengers left the Dutch-flagged MV Hondius in Tenerife after a hantavirus outbreak linked to at least three deaths, and the ship set sail for the Netherlands with a reduced crew and medical staff on board. Authorities said passengers and some crew members were flown or transferred to more than 20 countries for isolation or monitoring, while the vessel was expected to continue to Rotterdam for disinfection. Health agencies reported additional confirmed or suspected cases among evacuees, including an American and a French passenger, while the World Health Organization said the risk to the public remained low. Several countries ordered six-week or similar quarantines for passengers, crew and close contacts because health officials use 42 days as the maximum incubation period for the Andes hantavirus strain. The outbreak prompted an international containment operation involving Spain, the WHO, national health authorities and specialist quarantine units. Radboud University Medical Center in the Netherlands separately placed 12 workers in preventive quarantine after it said staff did not follow stricter procedures while taking blood samples and disposing of urine from an infected patient.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dBNqzDFtfOaz6BsQrp4hA8rRnVS41p8bdlXHqAww9clrV6eU4L6y6wLAKxgxhpeKdqvHYdrqV1lepsNxAZ86h_m1n55vMNdLoBTGJzgvwKnCkX2QMUVsugQk3sx5Mcwn5W1BmYE5LS5wlU7NpiKj4p8HNz9lEOOWYDM88eFqxKCvHMKKoVDJtsxlhLhkmR2GzsNhGC2FzF-jytse' alt='The cruise ship MV Hondius leaves the port of Granadilla de Abona, in Tenerife, Spain.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Case count: WHO figures cited after the evacuation listed nine linked cases, seven of them laboratory-confirmed, with one additional case under investigation after an inconclusive result.</li><li>U.S. monitoring: Seventeen Americans and one dual British-U.S. citizen arrived in the United States, with most taken to the University of Nebraska Medical Center and two sent to Atlanta for assessment or care.</li><li>French rules: France ordered reinforced hospital quarantine for identified close contacts after one repatriated French passenger tested positive and was treated in intensive care in Paris.</li><li>Final transfers: The last six people to leave included passengers initially expected to fly to Australia who were later put on the Netherlands-bound evacuation aircraft.</li></ul><blockquote>There is nothing to fear, the risk is low, this is not another COVID. - Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said keeping passengers aboard longer could have made the situation difficult and said the evacuation was preferable to extended confinement on the vessel. (<a href='https://www.manilatimes.net/2026/05/12/news/world/passengers-from-virus-stricken-cruise-ship-fly-to-home-countries-for-monitoring/2341204'>The Manila Times</a>)</li><li>Spain&#x27;s Health Ministry: Spain said it had taken all prevention and control measures to break possible transmission chains during the Tenerife evacuation, including health checks and supervised transfers. (<a href='https://www.manilatimes.net/2026/05/11/news/world/spain-says-took-all-measures-to-stop-hantavirus-after-positive-tests/2340973'>The Manila Times</a>)</li><li>Radboud University Medical Center: The Dutch hospital said patient care would continue uninterrupted and that it would investigate the incident to prevent similar lapses. (<a href='https://tribune.com.pk/story/2607650/planes-with-hantavirus-cruise-passengers-land-in-netherlands-hospital-quarantines-12'>The Express Tribune</a>)</li><li>MV Hondius captain Jan Dobrogowski: Dobrogowski issued a video message thanking passengers and crew for their conduct during the outbreak. (<a href='https://www.manilatimes.net/2026/05/12/news/world/passengers-from-virus-stricken-cruise-ship-fly-to-home-countries-for-monitoring/2341204'>The Manila Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><u